course: biology
lesson: 333
title: The garden in spring: buds, frogspawn, queen bumblebees and nesting birds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In spring a garden wakes up like a house on a school morning: everyone gets moving at once, and each has its own job to rush to.
explain: The horse chestnut's sticky buds burst open, a clump of frogspawn wobbles in the pond like jelly with black dots, and a huge furry queen bumblebee zigzags low over the lawn hunting for an old mouse hole to start her nest.
explain: Before sunrise the blackbird and the robin start singing to claim their patch, and soon a blackbird flies past with a beak full of moss and mud for her nest.
explain: Most of these spring jobs are about babies: eggs, nests and new families, so the kindest thing a person can do is watch from a little way off and help without getting in the way.
fact: Horse chestnut buds are covered in sticky stuff that keeps out insects and cold, and inside them the whole leaf and flower are packed tightly, ready to open.
fact: A cut horse chestnut twig kept in water indoors opens its buds early.
fact: A common frog lays about one to two thousand eggs in one clump, and only a few out of every thousand grow into adult frogs; frogs often come back to the pond where they grew up.
fact: A big bumblebee flying low in early spring is a queen, the only bumblebee that survives the winter; she searches for an old mouse hole or a tuft of grass to start a nest, which will soon hold dozens of workers.
fact: A bumblebee sitting still on a cold day may be cold or out of energy; moving it onto a flower is the best help, a little sugar water can be offered once, and honey must never be given because it can carry bee diseases.
fact: Blackbirds build cup-shaped nests of grass, moss and mud in hedges and bushes; the female builds and lays about three to five blue-green eggs with brown speckles.
fact: In Britain it is against the law to disturb a wild bird's nest while it is in use, so people watch from far away and avoid cutting hedges from March to August.
fact: Daffodils and snowdrops flower before the trees have leaves, using food stored in their bulbs to catch sunlight before the trees shade the ground; daffodil bulbs look like onions but are poisonous.
fact: Male birds sing at dawn to claim their patch and attract a partner; the still, quiet air at dawn carries song far, and robins and blackbirds are often among the first to start.
fact: A primrose has five pale yellow petals with a darker centre that guides insects to the nectar, and its name comes from old words meaning «first rose».
fact: Hedgehogs wake from hibernation thin and hungry; a shallow dish of water and meaty cat food helps them, while milk and bread are bad for them.
exercise: Why are horse chestnut buds sticky? | The sticky stuff keeps out insects and cold | The tree is leaking sweet honey out of its twigs | So birds can stick to the branches | To catch rain for the leaves
exercise: What is packed inside a horse chestnut bud? | The whole new leaf and flower, ready to open | A tiny conker, waiting to grow big by autumn | Only water | A sleeping insect
exercise: What happens if you put a cut horse chestnut twig in water indoors? | Its buds open early | It grows roots and a conker at once | Its buds fall off | It turns into a new tree in a week
exercise: About how many eggs does a common frog lay in one clump? | One to two thousand | About ten | Exactly one hundred | About a million
exercise: Why does a frog lay so many eggs? | Only a few out of every thousand grow into adult frogs | The pond needs filling up with jelly before the summer comes | Frogs cannot count | All of them grow into frogs
exercise: Which bumblebee survives the winter? | Only the queen | Only the workers | Only the males | All of them, asleep in the nest
exercise: What is a big bumblebee flying low over the grass in early spring doing? | Looking for an old mouse hole or a grass tuft for a nest | Looking for its old hive from last year, where its sisters slept all winter | Looking for snow to drink | Chasing other bees out of the garden
exercise: What is the best help for a still, tired bumblebee on the path? | Move it gently onto a flower | Give it a spoon of honey | Put it in the fridge to rest | Pour water over it
exercise: Why must a tired bee never be given honey? | Honey can carry bee diseases | Honey is too sweet for bees | Bees cannot eat anything sticky | Honey makes bees sleep for a week
exercise: What does a blackbird use to build its nest? | Grass, moss and mud | Twigs glued with spit only | Feathers and plastic | Stones and sand
exercise: What do blackbird eggs look like? | Blue-green with brown speckles | Plain white and round | Bright red with stripes | Black with yellow spots
exercise: Why do people avoid cutting hedges from March to August? | To protect birds' nests | Because hedges cannot be cut in warm weather | Because the leaves are poisonous then | Because it is against the law to garden in summer
exercise: Why do daffodils and snowdrops flower so early? | To catch sunlight before the trees grow leaves and shade the ground | Because bees only fly in winter, and the flowers must be ready for them | Because they like snow best | Because they die if it gets warm
exercise: Why do male birds sing at dawn? | To claim their patch and attract a partner | Because the dark scares them and singing keeps them brave | Because they are hungry | To wake up the people
exercise: How many petals does a primrose usually have? | Five | Three | Seven | Twelve
exercise: What should you put out for a hungry hedgehog in spring? | Water and meaty cat food | Milk and bread | Apples and cake | Nothing, they eat only grass
test: «found a massive bee on the path, not moving, gonna put a bit of honey on a spoon for it» What is better? | Move it onto a flower; honey can spread bee diseases | Put it in a jar with a lid to keep it warm for a week | Honey is perfect, give it lots | Wake it with cold water
test: «frogs lay like 2000 eggs, the pond's gonna be FULL of frogs lol» What is right? | Only a few out of every thousand will grow into frogs | Every egg becomes a frog, so yes, the pond will be full by summer | Frogs eat all their eggs | The eggs turn into fish instead
test: «there's a nest in the hedge, dad wants it gone so he can trim, sod the birds» What is right in Britain? | It is against the law to disturb a nest in use; wait until the young have left | Nests can be moved to another bush, and the birds will follow it there at once | Trimming is fine if it is quick | Birds rebuild in an hour, so it does not matter
test: Why might a bird leave its nest if people keep coming too close? | It may think the nest is not safe from danger | Birds cannot stand the smell of grass | People's shadows freeze the eggs | Birds only nest where no one lives
test: A little brother finds a daffodil bulb and says «onion! let's fry it». What is right? | Daffodil bulbs look like onions but are poisonous | Daffodil bulbs taste just like onions | Only the flowers are poisonous | It is fine if you cook it well
test: Why is early spring a good time for low flowers? | The trees have no leaves yet, so sunlight reaches the ground | The soil is warmest in early spring, warmer than in summer | There is no rain in spring | Bees only visit low flowers
test: In a cartoon a thin hedgehog wakes up and Mum gives it a bowl of milk and bread. What is right? | Water and cat food are good; milk and bread are bad for hedgehogs | Milk and bread are the best hedgehog food after the long winter sleep | Hedgehogs only drink warm tea | Hedgehogs do not eat after waking
test: Why would a hedgehog wake up thin in spring? | It lived for months on its stored fat without eating | It ran about all winter in the snow looking for slugs | It gave all its food to frogs | Hedgehogs always lose weight in the sun
test: Why would a nest need mud as well as grass? | Mud holds the grass together like glue and makes the cup strong | Mud keeps the eggs cold | Mud is food for the chicks | Mud makes the nest invisible at night
test: Why do birds sing more in spring than in autumn? | Spring is when they claim patches and find partners for nesting | Birds lose their voices in autumn, when the cold air hurts their throats | The air is too thick in autumn | Birds are too sleepy in spring to be quiet
test: Count them: 3 frogs sit on the edge of the pond and 2 blackbirds hop on the lawn. How many legs are there? | 16 | 10 | 12 | 20
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a common frog deposits a very large number of eggs in a single clump. | Only a small proportion of the eggs survive to become adult frogs | All of the eggs are certain to mature into adult frogs | The eggs are required to fill the pond with jelly | The female frog is unable to regulate the number of eggs owing to the cold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a large bumblebee observed flying close to the ground in early spring? | It is a queen, the sole survivor of the winter, in search of a site for a new nest | It is a worker returning to a hive occupied throughout the winter | It is a male in search of honey stored from the previous year | It is an insect of a species unrelated to bumblebees
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nests of wild birds in Britain. | The disturbance of a nest while it is in use is prohibited by law | The removal of a nest is permitted provided it is undertaken swiftly | Nests may lawfully be relocated to any adjacent shrub | The law applies exclusively to the nests of birds of prey
exam: Ascertain the advantage obtained by snowdrops and daffodils in flowering before the trees are in leaf. | The reception of sunlight before the ground is shaded by the canopy | The avoidance of all insect visitors | The absorption of water from the snow alone | The protection of the bulbs from frost by means of the flowers
